# DURABLE REPRO — `E_SpawnWithBorrowedRef` appears to be a DEAD GUARD.
#
# `docs/book/14-concurrency.md` §3.9 documents this program as rejected
# ("ERROR: borrowed reference crossing spawn boundary"). At HEAD it is
# ACCEPTED: `gg check` reports "OK: no semantic errors".
#
# WRONG TODAY (measured at HEAD, Rust gg). Four shapes probed, all accepted:
#   `&`-of-a-local        `spawn child(&cfg)`        (this fixture)
#   `&`-of-a-`&`-param    parent(Config &cfg) -> `spawn child(&cfg)`
#   owned String param    `spawn worker(data)`
#   `&`-param collection  launch(Vector[int] &data) -> `spawn worker(data)`
#
# ⚠ THE GUARD HAS NO POSITIVE CONTROL (Core #13). Every reference to
# `SemanticErrorKind::SpawnWithBorrowedRef` in `src/semantic/safety/tests.rs`
# is a NEGATIVE assertion (`!has_error`) — not one test asserts it FIRES. Two
# of them were even NAMED `..._rejected` while asserting acceptance; their
# bodies explain why ("With StringView removed, String params are owned, not
# borrowed"). So when StringView was removed the borrow-origin shapes this
# check was written for stopped existing, the assertions were flipped, and the
# names were left behind. Renamed to `..._accepted` at the same commit as this
# fixture.
#
# The raise sites still exist — `helpers.rs:1695` (args) and
# `check_expr.rs:933` (method receiver) — so this is "no reachable shape
# found", NOT "the code was deleted". Establishing which of the two it is
# is the work; that is why this is filed rather than fixed inline.
#
# INTENDED: one of two answers, and the round that closes this must pick.
#   (a) The guard is LIVE and the reachable shape simply was not found — then
#       this fixture graduates to a NEG fixture asserting the rejection, and a
#       positive-control unit test lands beside it.
#   (b) The guard is DEAD because escape-safety now belongs to the closure
#       capture/escape analysis (D34) — then the raise sites and the error
#       variant are DELETED, and §3.9 is rewritten to describe whatever
#       actually protects the spawn boundary. A safety rule documented in the
#       book with nothing enforcing it is a Core #14 defect either way.
#
# ⚠ THIS FIXTURE ASSERTS NEITHER OUTCOME. It is a probe: it records that the
# documented rejection does not happen. Do not read its acceptance as a pin on
# the accept side.
#
# ORACLE (ggdef): OUT OF SUBSET — ggdef has no `spawn`/`Task`. Recorded per
# Core #9.
#
# The seed is COMPUTED, not a literal, so no const-fold can elide the call.
#
# Cited from TODO.md (safety / spawn / Medium).

struct Config:
    int n

int seed():
    return 1

void child(Config &cfg):
    cfg.n = cfg.n + 1

void parent():
    Config cfg = Config(seed())
    spawn child(&cfg)

void main():
    parent()
    print(0)
